Who am I to judge?
As of now, 1000 hours of gameplay (not counting pre-steam period), account created 3.5 years ago, have basically tried every class in the game (not every nation though), so far have only spent around 20 euros on the game (sorry devs, I am still a college student), most of the time obviously playing without membership, have watched the game develop significantly. Oh... and I really do like the game (slightly biased).
Positives:
+ 3 nations, 5 classes to chose from, something for everyone (planes, tanks, sniper rifles, mines, you name it...);
+ Some really unique features: paratrooper gameplay, all classes fighting in one battle, war matches set up by players etc.;
+ You get good by learning. Differentiate gun sounds, know reload times, sniping spots, tactics and so on.
+ Decent graphics, neat animations, detailed gun mechanics, runs pretty good (nothing too impressive here though);
+ Well balanced nations, every nation has some unique features as well;
+ NOT P2W! Everything in the game can be bought for in-game currency, even membership! Of course you can invest money if you want to progress faster. Membership affects only your income, not performance (except only for the 2nd combat bagde);
+ Unlockable content for a long time, you won't be "completing" the game that soon;
+ Developers actually care. Regular devstreams, they do read forums and Reddit page, they also tweak updates after release basing it on feedback;
+ Nice balance between realism and simple gameplay;
+ Game still has a huge potential and a long way ahead. I actually get hyped when a new update comes out;
+ Cheating is almost non-existant;
+ Gives that good old Medal of Honor: Allied Assault nostalgia (this is for older gamers out there)
Negatives:
- Game is very harsh to newcomers. Start with basic weapon, small income, little knowledge (tutorial should be expanded), get thrown into "big world" fairly soon. That's where a chunk of negative reviews come from;
- Global map (generals gameplay) needs some developing for a longer time;
- I personally like the grind, but many may find it to be the opposite. I like the game, therefore I don't mind playing it on and on. But yes, you will grind for everything, if you want all at once, this isn't a game for you;
- Development is regular, but still slow;
- In-game economics are basic and pretty rough. I have accumulated 2 millions without special effort, but there is always ways to spend that money (another weapon, another camo, another soldier). Money comes hard but goes easy.
- Engine is just a bit clumsy for gunplay. This isn't quite like the Source engine (CS:GO), requires getting used to.
- Trolling is limited. There is no in-game voice chat (I actually find this to be okay to avoid European multi-linguistic blabber), taunts etc.
Additional important points:
* Keep in mind that devteam is around 30 people. Can't ask from them the same you ask from big companies.
* Solo gameplay is one thing, but I suggest you spice it up with friends, especially when starting up (it's funnier when you have bad weapons together).
* When just starting, I suggest you visit H&G wiki or reddit and do some reading. It will help you a lot. Don't rush it.
* Don't give it up after 10 hours of gameplay. Start is hard, but get a pair of good weapons, get to know the game and see what you think then. I also quit soon after starting but returned after almost a year. Haven't left since.
* Don't rush into war matches. Those are for experienced players. Get some skills in staged matches first.
In conclusion, I give this game 9/10. Mostly because it is my current favorite and also it has future, because as the time goes it only gets better, contrary to other games where devs actually ♥♥♥♥ it up.
Cheers! And please, guys with 10 hours under your belt, avoid writing reviews, you haven't seen♥♥♥♥♥♥yet! H&G delivers in long term.
